From: Kervin Pursoty <kpursoty@proton.me>
Subject: clocksource: timer-econet-en751221: fix IRQ ack to disarm compare register

cevt_interrupt() acks the compare register by writing the current count
value back to compare:

  iowrite32(ioread32(reg_count(cpu)), reg_compare(cpu));

On EN751627 this is a posted write through KSEG1 (uncached). By the time
the write completes, the free-running counter has already advanced past
the written value, so the pending bit re-asserts immediately. The result
is a tight interrupt storm on CPU0 that starves all other IRQs, including
QDMA (ethernet) which never gets a chance to deliver.

Fix: ack by writing U32_MAX to compare (effectively disarming it), then
let the next set_next_event() arm it with the correct future value. This
is consistent with how cevt_dev_init() initialises the register.

Signed-off-by: Kervin Pursoty <kpursoty@proton.me>
--- a/drivers/clocksource/timer-econet-en751221.c
+++ b/drivers/clocksource/timer-econet-en751221.c
@@ -85,7 +85,8 @@
 	if (!cevt_is_pending(cpu))
 		return IRQ_NONE;

-	iowrite32(ioread32(reg_count(cpu)), reg_compare(cpu));
+	/* Disarm compare to prevent re-fire on posted-write; set_next_event() re-arms. */
+	iowrite32(U32_MAX, reg_compare(cpu));
 	dev->event_handler(dev);
 	return IRQ_HANDLED;
 }
